  "slug": "evolutionary-rescue-adaptation-0377",
  "summary": "New experimental-evolution and introduction studies probe why some populations adapt fast enough to persist while others, despite adaptation, go extinct.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how genetic architecture and contingency determine whether populations adapt quickly enough to persist. In cowpea seed beetles, rapid and repeatable evolutionary rescue on lentil depends on large non-recombining haploblocks on chromosomes 1 and 9. In outdoor Drosophila under insecticide stress, half of the populations went extinct despite strong parallel polygenic adaptation, and pre-adaptation prevented extinction. Replicated stickleback introductions across nine lakes show that source population identity predicts success better than environmental matching. Drosophila suzukii is tested for seasonal adaptive tracking as a driver of invasion. Boechera stricta genome–environment analyses suggest projected hot, dry climates may exceed adaptive capacity. A second strand addresses genetic load: simulations indicate that reproductive competition amplifies purifying selection, and a review links deleterious mutations to crop breeding and conservation. Mosquito models show standing variation evolving against a suppression gene drive and altering its outcomes. What is new is the joint weighing of structural variation, polygenic parallelism, source effects, and the limits of predicting persistence.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
